t are very very low and i  don't walk around with any anxiety about  it myself however what they do in terms  of the mood and the fearfulness  and the way they provide a litmus test  about how much do gentiles react is  significant so for example i think  there's definitely an anti-jewish bias  in a lot of the mainstream media if you  look at the way that collegeville  collieville was reported in texas it got  far less attention than it would have if  a different if any different minority  group had been um held hostage in their  house of worship there's just no  question that jews don't count as much  um at many media outlets so it's very  complicated  and you know to follow up on what you  were saying about the orthodox um you  know it strikes me that you know there  there are constant attacks on orthodox  jews now i mean i just read about you  know more this week and there is not  that same  um  kumbaya kind of outpouring of  inter-faith i mean or much less than it  seemed no there's not and indeed yeah  and not  not even from other jews i mean the real  the real shonda the real shame there is  what i see as the secular and liberal  jewish um willingness to throw our more  observant brethren and cistern if that's  a word under the bus um that when it's  black-hated jews and their and their  wives um being um  uh  you know  punched and kicked and attacked verbally  and physically in borough park  that um  that  less observant and more acculturated  assimilated jews several miles away  um don't care that much and that's a  that's um  you know is that is that anti-semitism  that's  i mean yeah there's some self-loathing  there's some shame i mean the the  anti-orthodox animus among many  non-orthodox jews is profound and deep  it exists in my own family  and um  you know we're a family of non-orthodox  jews  for the most part all jews have some  relatives who are orthodox um  and i think that that's a that's a  conversation internal conversation that  we we have to 