ld meet orthodox who  said well my grandpa was reform but two  generations later i've gotten more  observant but they stayed in squirrel  hill the whole time so the family ties  go back very deep  i i would just say you know you practice  hospitality and make sure it's a  hospitality toward jews who are not like  you as well as jews who are  it's very simple but it's very hard to  do and most of us including me fall down  on the job  um  yeah so a question about um  how you know what people have done in  squirrel hill but also maybe just more  generally to counter the source of the  tragedy which is the resurgence of of  white nationalism and even outright  nazism uh  can you say a little bit more i mean  they're on the very kind of basic level  there are security measures that you  know most synagogues now have a guard we  didn't used to mostly have a guard my  synagogue now is a good guard i  am a skeptic about how much that would  matter if a well-armed person wanted to  come and kill jews  uh i think  um  i think it might not matter much um the  guard might just be the first person to  get shot after which it might be open  season so um you know i've talked to a  lot of friends who have other ideas  about what security would work rather  than a kind of security theater that i  think we now have which often by the way  proves very expensive a lot of money is  being wasted on bogus security matters  right now um  but  um  in terms of  you know how to counter the ideology i  don't think dudes can counter the i  don't think anyone  jews can't fix anti-semites anti-semites  are soul sick people  um and they're not reacting to jews and  they're not reacting to israel they're  they're  um and they're not reacting to  immigration  they are you know um it's not because to  even go down that line of thinking i  think suggests that there's a kind of  rationale  to anti-semitism there is of course an  internal logic to it in their own brains  it makes some sense but irrational  bigotry um  defeats um  that kind of i mean it's like it's like  cult deprogramming can't be done sure  but it's extremely labor intensive  you know and it's i mean people should  go read the terrific book elizabeth's  book rising out of hatred um about the  former clansman derek black who went to  new college florida where he ended up  leaving he'd been raised in a kind of  clan family very prominent clan family  again the book is zaslow saslo sas low  and it's called rising out of hatred i  think he's a washington post reporter  the best book i've read and you can give  it to an 11 year old like it reads very  easily and my daughter's read it um  totally gripping and this guy went off  to college for some reason he went to a  fairly progressive liberal arts college  and some jews started inviting him to  shabbat dinner  and that's actually what cured him of  his anti-semitism was just knowing jews  so i think we have to practice more  hospitality to all humans but we should  do that anyway because it's a mitzvah  that we're called on to do i don't think  we can worry much about how to cure  anti-semites  okay great well that brings us exactly  to the end of our program so i just want  to thank you so much um on behalf of  everyone who you can't see who i'm sure  are they're all silently clapping at  home um thank you i urge everyone to to  buy the book and um really thank you  again mark uh it was really a  fascinating discussion so well  you and and the community around you  have been a huge source of support and  i'm excited to meet everyone in person  when when that's possible so thanks  again exactly okay and thank you to  everyone  who showed up  bye-bye
 

